// Day 20: Registry Run / RunOnce key writes on the lab endpoint
//
// What it looks for: values written under CurrentVersion\Run and CurrentVersion\RunOnce
// (the match also catches variants such as RunOnceEx). Windows reads these keys at logon
// and starts whatever they point to. ATT&CK T1547.001.
//
// Why it matters: Run keys are one of the most common persistence mechanisms. The useful
// fields are RegistryValueData (what will run) and InitiatingProcessCommandLine (who wrote it).
//
// Result in my lab (Atomic Red Team T1547.001 tests 1-3): two rows.
//   4:01:55 PM  HKCU Run      "Atomic Red Team" = C:\Path\AtomicRedTeam.exe   (written by REG ADD)
//   4:01:59 PM  HKLM RunOnce  "NextRun" = a PowerShell command that downloads and runs Discovery.bat
//
// Caveat: not every registry write shows up in this table. The RunOnceEx write from test 2
// had no row here. See reg-exe-runonce-process.kql.
DeviceRegistryEvents
| where DeviceName =~ "mdf-dt-w11"
| where RegistryKey contains "CurrentVersion\\Run"
| project Timestamp, RegistryKey, RegistryValueName, RegistryValueData, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
